    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5ec77ea8-f483-53d1-ad1a-2c42eedb5d1a",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + 2 x + 1$ at $\\left(-1,1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} + 2 x + 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 2\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=4 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=-2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-2\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}\\\\\n\\left(-2\\right)y&=-x+\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-2\\right)y&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-2$ and the constant is $-3$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e102e700-fb58-5cbc-9439-19f06eafd572",
          ":answer-field/key": "coefficient",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
