    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aef3a3f0-c638-5589-8161-4b6d69c14771",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x + 2$ at $\\left(2,6\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - 2 x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 2\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=4 \\left(2\\right) - 2=6\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(6\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(6\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{6}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(6\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{19}{3} - \\frac{x}{6}\\\\\n\\left(6\\right)y&=-x+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(36\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(6\\right)y&=38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $6$ and the constant is $38$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
