    "question": {
      ":question/id": "92578129-b5d4-52fa-b720-c6bb357472e9",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^{3} - x - 1$ at $\\left(1,-1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=x^{3} - x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3 x^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1=2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-1\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\\\\n\\left(2\\right)y&=-x+\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(2\\right)y&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $2$ and the constant is $-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
