  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b6f4e697-cc59-522b-94f9-839188a75a30",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{3} - x - 2$ at $\\left(-1,1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- 2 x^{3} - x - 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 6 x^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=- 6 \\left(-1\\right)^{2} - 1=-7\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-7\\right)}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=\\frac{1}{7}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{7}+\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{7} + \\frac{8}{7}\\\\\n\\left(-7\\right)y&=-x+\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-7\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-7\\right)y&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-7$ and the constant is $-8$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "28d9d186-bf49-52d8-bcf4-46a7102eb5e2",
