    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ec862934-fcb9-5204-bd35-f282a1d2dfef",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - x - 1$ at $\\left(1,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=4 \\left(1\\right) - 1=3\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(3\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{3}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{x}{3}\\\\\n\\left(3\\right)y&=-x+\\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(3\\right)y&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $3$ and the constant is $1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "31b7c685-7543-5422-bb52-88233e9c0695",
