  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0a557cc4-7e24-5b85-83ad-1312fbb93c89",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + x^{3}$ at $\\left(2,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + x^{3}$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- \\frac{3 x^{5}}{4} + 3 x^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\left(2\\right)^{5}}{4} + 3 \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-12\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-12\\right)}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=\\frac{1}{12}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{12}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{12} - \\frac{1}{6}\\\\\n\\left(-12\\right)y&=-x+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-12\\right)y&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-12$ and the constant is $2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ff39b424-912d-5f10-8b5d-f9b633b8d841",
