    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a554144c-ad25-5e5e-8b53-18068b763104",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + x$ at $\\left(2,10\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} + x$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=4 \\left(2\\right) + 1=9\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(9\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(10\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{9}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{9}+\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)+\\left(10\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{92}{9} - \\frac{x}{9}\\\\\n\\left(9\\right)y&=-x+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(90\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(9\\right)y&=92\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $9$ and the constant is $92$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4e0fc05a-194e-52ee-b372-c4983fa831ee",
