    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dd206954-4ae2-5694-be7b-900c46683c48",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^{2} - x + 2$ at $\\left(0,2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=x^{2} - x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(0\\right)=2 \\left(0\\right) - 1=-1\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-1\\right)}=1.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(2\\right)&=1\\left(x-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=x+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\ny&=x + 2\\\\\n\\left(-1\\right)y&=-x+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-1\\right)y&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-1$ and the constant is $-2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8591dcc5-49c6-5346-b1b9-7e25021dba76",
