  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7bed0381-4342-5442-beae-842cad8ea0c7",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + \\frac{7 x^{3}}{4} - 1$ at $\\left(2,5\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + \\frac{7 x^{3}}{4} - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- \\frac{3 x^{5}}{4} + \\frac{21 x^{2}}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\left(2\\right)^{5}}{4} + \\frac{21 \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{4}=-3\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-3\\right)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(5\\right)&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{3}+\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(5\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{13}{3}\\\\\n\\left(-3\\right)y&=-x+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(-15\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-3\\right)y&=-13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-3$ and the constant is $-13$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "aabef8c8-fc9e-5e49-b676-114760f36fba",
