    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e182a472-2471-5a58-9e42-b3d4a98d83f0",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{3} - x$ at $\\left(1,1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{3} - x$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=6 x^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=6 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1=5\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(5\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{5}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{5}+\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{x}{5}\\\\\n\\left(5\\right)y&=-x+\\left(1\\right)+\\left(5\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(5\\right)y&=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $5$ and the constant is $6$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
