    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8865a5b9-fd2d-5afe-b0a2-38162e5b1f17",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{3} + x + 2$ at $\\left(1,2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- x^{3} + x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=1 - 3 x^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=1 - 3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}=-2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-2\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(2\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}\\\\\n\\left(-2\\right)y&=-x+\\left(1\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-2\\right)y&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-2$ and the constant is $-3$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
